{"moat":{"rating":"narrow","sources_of_moat":["Global brand equity (Nike, Jordan, Converse) with scale. Nike is the largest athletic footwear and apparel seller, with an estimated 27–30% share of athletic footwear against roughly 11–13% for Adidas.","Scale in marketing and athlete and league endorsements. Endorsement commitments are about $15.5B, which smaller rivals cannot match. The NFL uniform and licensing deal runs through 2038.","Performance-sport innovation. The Performance portfolio was about $16B in FY26 and grew high single digits in Q1 FY27. Running is gaining share, and Nike says its max-cushioning share nearly tripled.","North America strength and a broad wholesale and direct footprint, plus first-party consumer data through the Nike app and SNKRS.","Supply-chain scale, though production is outsourced and concentrated in Vietnam, Indonesia and China."],"evidence":"The moat is real but has eroded and consumer switching costs are negligible. Revenue fell from $51.4B in FY24 to $46.4B in FY26. Gross margin dropped from 44.6% to 42.9% and EBIT margin from 12.7% to 8.3%. FCF fell from $6.6B to $2.2B. ROIC is still 18.7% on the company's definition, down from 20.2%. Q1 FY27 revenue fell 4%: Sportswear fell low double digits after Dunk supply was cut nearly 50%, Jordan fell mid-teens, and Greater China fell 26%. On, Hoka, Adidas and New Balance are taking share, and Nike's apparel share fell about 30 bps in 2025. Nike is still far larger than any rival. The analysis rests on single-point figures that are unverified, so I am treating it as narrow and not wide. Sources: https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nike-q1-earnings-call-highlights-000223968.html ; https://www.companieshistory.com/nike-swot-analysis/ ; https://www.fool.com/earnings/call-transcripts/2026/10/02/nike-nke-q1-2027-earnings-call-transcript/"},"management":{"rating":"average","capital_allocation":"Dividends paid of $2.4B in FY26 exceeded FCF of $2.18B. Q1 FY27 FCF was about -$64M, so the payout is not covered by free cash flow in the current trough. Buybacks have been paused since Q1 FY26. The program bought 124.4M shares at an average of $97.57 (about $12.1B) against a current price of about $33, which destroyed value. The balance sheet is adequate: $8.4B of cash and short-term investments, net cash, A+/A2 ratings (both downgraded in 2025), and no credit facility borrowings. CEO Elliott Hill returned in October 2024 and has reorganized the company by sport, rebuilt wholesale relationships and cut inventory. The new 'Pace' program targets about $2.5B of cumulative savings through FY31 against about $1.0B of charges, on top of about $0.3B of FY26 severance. FY27 guidance is for revenue down high single digits, with EBIT down by more than that. Adjusted EPS guidance of $1.15–$1.35 comes from a single secondary source and is unverified. Execution is still unproven: the reset has run through FY27 and the Sportswear and Jordan actions extend beyond it. Sources: https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nike-q1-earnings-call-highlights-000223968.html ; https://ca.investing.com/news/company-news/nike-inc-nke-q1-2027-earnings-call-highlights-performance-gains-offset-by-china-reset-and--4863264 ; https://finance.biggo.com/news/US_NKE_2026-10-01 ; https://about.nike.com/en/magazine/elliott-hill-ceo-of-sport-interview","governance_concerns":["Dual-class structure. Class A stock is held mainly by Swoosh, LLC, the entity co-founder Phil Knight formed in 2015 to hold most of his Class A shares. Class A holders elect their own set of directors. Voting power is concentrated with the founder, which limits Class B shareholders' influence. Nike's proxy says some investors have raised specific concerns about the structure. Proxy: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000320187/000032018726000089/nikecourtesypdf2026.pdf","The proxy says three of the eight Class A nominees are not independent. This is the proxy's own disclosure, so the count is not unverified.","Nike's prior strategy under CEO John Donahoe (direct-to-consumer push, weakened wholesale ties) is blamed for the current reset. The Donahoe-era strategy led to his abrupt departure in 2024. The board's oversight of that period is a governance question.","Stock decline of nearly 78% (per WWD) took Nike out of the S&P 100 in September 2026. Shareholders still re-elected all 11 director nominees. Source: https://wwd.com/footwear-news/shoe-industry-news/nike-annual-shareholder-meeting-ceo-elliott-hill-progress-1239201228/","Stock-based compensation was $715M in FY26, about 23% of net income.","Hill is a Nike insider of more than 32 years. The new CFO, Dave Denton, is also new in the role. Both are positives for culture but add key-person and groupthink risk. No specific accounting or related-party issues were found in the search results."]},"industry":{"growth_outlook":"Industry growth is moderate. One 2025 source puts global sportswear growth at about 7%, which is unverified. Growth is shifting toward performance running, tennis, golf and football, and away from the lifestyle categories where Nike is shrinking. Nike's own near-term growth is negative: FY27 revenue is guided down high single digits, with Greater China expected to worsen over the rest of the year. Management expects most reset actions to be done by about December 2026. Sportswear and Jordan actions will run longer, and Converse fell 28% in Q1. Long-run Nike growth from this depressed base is possible, but its timing is unknown.","competitive_intensity":"High. Consumer switching costs are low and fashion cycles are fast. On and Hoka are taking share in premium running, and Lululemon in women's training and athleisure. Adidas is growing strongly (+14% currency-neutral in Q1 2026) on its terrace-shoe styles. New Balance and Skechers are gaining. Anta competes in China. Tariffs and the concentration of production in Asia add cost pressure. Nike's North American wholesale partners such as Dick's and JD Sports are again important. Sources: https://www.companieshistory.com/adidas-competitors ; https://www.growthnavigate.com/nike-competitors ; https://logos-pres.md/en/news/nike-loses-market-share-and-competitors-gain-ground/","key_competitors":["Adidas","On Holding","Deckers (Hoka)","New Balance","Lululemon","Puma","Skechers","Anta Sports","Under Armour","Asics"]},"catalysts":["Completion of the marketplace reset (supply cuts, digital repositioned to full price, markdown liquidation), expected for most segments by about December 2026. Sportswear and Jordan actions will extend beyond FY27.","Continued share gains in running and performance categories (Vomero, Pegasus Plus, Alphafly 4). Basketball is also growing, with the Caitlin Clark signature launch in about 5,000 doors.","North America wholesale recovery, with North America revenue up 2% in Q1 FY27.","Pace cost program: about $2.5B of cumulative savings through FY31, against about $1.0B of charges (about $0.3B in FY27). Savings are stated before reinvestment.","Gross margin: up 60 bps to 42.8% in Q1 FY27, with a possible tailwind from tariff refunds (about $684M of IEEPA refunds collected after year-end, per the filings analysis).","Greater China stabilization and the first China-made-for-China collection launching in October 2026. Management says China revenue will worsen before it improves.","Sports events and league partnerships, including the NFL deal through 2038.","Resumption of share repurchases (about $5.9B authorized) once FCF recovers. This is unlikely near term.","Fiscal 2028 reorganization into three geographies (Americas, APGC, AMAYA)."],"citations":["https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nike-q1-earnings-call-highlights-000223968.html","https://ca.investing.com/news/company-news/nike-inc-nke-q1-2027-earnings-call-highlights-performance-gains-offset-by-china-reset-and--4863264","https://www.fool.com/earnings/call-transcripts/2026/10/02/nike-nke-q1-2027-earnings-call-transcript/","https://gloom.sh/stocks/nke/transcripts/q1-2027","https://finance.biggo.com/news/US_NKE_2026-10-01","https://www.tradingkey.com/news/transcripts/262197031-tradingkey","https://www.companieshistory.com/nike-swot-analysis/","https://www.companieshistory.com/adidas-competitors","https://www.growthnavigate.com/nike-competitors","https://logos-pres.md/en/news/nike-loses-market-share-and-competitors-gain-ground/","https://swotpal.com/blog/nike-swot-analysis-2026","https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000320187/000032018726000089/nikecourtesypdf2026.pdf","https://about.nike.com/en/magazine/elliott-hill-ceo-of-sport-interview","https://wwd.com/footwear-news/shoe-industry-news/nike-annual-shareholder-meeting-ceo-elliott-hill-progress-1239201228/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliott_Hill"]}
