 "why_questionable": "The bear case still starts from the same inflated $4.0B base. It never tests a scenario with FCF at or below $2.18B, FY27 FCF below FY26, a dividend cut, or China and Converse deterioration. The bear case is therefore not a real bear case, and the 'reliable: true' flag with empty notes understates the model risk."
 },
 {
 "assumption": "5% growth from a trough base",
 "why_questionable": "There is no evidence of a trough. Q1 FCF was negative, revenue is guided down high single digits, and the CFO and COO are new. FY28 brings a reorganization into three geographies. The positives listed (North America, Pace savings) are partly offset by the negatives in the same list. Base FCF growth is also mixing two things: normalization and growth."
 },
